  [FORMULA RATIONALE]:
    The 10% reef-attributable share is a conservative proxy derived from Spalding et al. (2017) to isolate reef-dependent tourism expenditure from general coastal tourism. In the Malaysian context, this coefficient accounts for the portion of visitor spending directly linked to reef-based activities (diving, snorkeling, and reef-viewing) within the broader domestic tourism ecosystem.
  [ECONOMIC BENCHMARKING]:
    The RM60.09M/year reef-adjacent spending proxy represents annual cash flow for 30 monitoring units. This is distinct from the RM8.7 Billion/year Total Economic Value (TEV) benchmark established by the DMPM (2011-2015) study, which captures the broader non-market existence and bequest value of marine biodiversity across six major archipelagos.
  [LIMITATIONS]:
    1. The 10% coefficient is a static global estimate and may not fully capture site-specific variations in reef-dependency for islands like Perhentian.
2. The model currently lacks granular, island-specific expenditure data to refine the attribution coefficient.
3. Future iterations require localized survey data to validate if the 10% share accurately reflects the Malaysian domestic tourism expenditure profile.
  [CITATIONS]: DOSM Domestic Tourism Survey 2024, Marine biodiversity economic valuation

