=== [Judge Iter 0056] 2026-09-16 13:19:09 | Rubric Focus: Feature Engineering ===
Judge Question: "Explain the mathematics of Tree-Path Decomposition (Saabas 2014) in deriving unit-level stress contributions."
Context: Island=Redang, Tab=overview
Model Defense (2.69s) | Title: Stress Factor Attribution Methodology | Pose: answer | Links: 2
  [CONCEPT]:
    ReefSafe utilizes Tree-Path Decomposition (Saabas 2014) to interpret the Gradient Boosting Regressor. This method decomposes the model's prediction by tracing the decision path of each monitoring unit through every tree in the ensemble.
  [MATHEMATICAL DERIVATION]:
    1. The contribution of a feature is calculated as the change in the expected value of the target variable (live coral cover change) as the model traverses from the root node to the leaf node.
2. For each split, the contribution is the difference between the expected value of the child node and the parent node, weighted by the sample distribution.
3. The sum of these contributions across all trees in the ensemble provides the total feature importance for a specific unit, such as Redang.
4. This approach isolates the impact of substrate, fish counts, and pollution indicators from the baseline model prediction.
  [MODEL PERFORMANCE]:
    The Gradient Boosting Regressor achieves a Mean Absolute Error (MAE) of 5.862 pp/yr, with a 95% pooled forward-test residual distribution ranging from -16.25 to +13.66 pp/yr.
  [CITATIONS]: Reef Check Malaysia annual reports, OpenDOSM Data Catalogue

