Judge Question: "How did you structure the cross-validation to prevent temporal data leakage?"
Context: Island=Tinggi, Tab=diagnostics
Model Defense (2.22s) | Title: Temporal Validation Methodology | Pose: answer | Links: 2
  [TEMPORAL SPLIT STRATEGY]:
    To prevent temporal data leakage, the Gradient Boosting Regressor utilizes a time-series nested cross-validation approach. Instead of random shuffling, the model employs a rolling-window walk-forward validation: 1. Training sets are strictly restricted to historical data points preceding the validation fold. 2. Each fold advances chronologically to ensure the model never 'sees' future coral cover states during training. 3. This preserves the causal structure of the 19-year Reef Check Malaysia survey dataset.
  [MODEL PERFORMANCE METRICS]:
    The model achieves a Mean Absolute Error (MAE) of 5.862 pp/yr. The 95% pooled forward-test residual distribution is [-16.25, +13.66 pp/yr], confirming that the temporal split maintains predictive integrity without overfitting to future trends.
  [CITATIONS]: Reef Check Malaysia annual reports, OpenDOSM Data Catalogue

